{"city":{"id":"melbourne-bayside","name":"Melbourne Bayside","country":"Australia","timezone":"Australia/Melbourne","locale":"en-AU","description":"Port Phillip Bay from St Kilda to Brighton: the pier and the breakwater's little penguins, Luna Park, the Elwood foreshore and the Brighton bathing boxes, from open data under the real sun.","vertical_datum":"m AHD","year":2026,"tour":[{"title":"Port Phillip, St Kilda to Brighton","text":"{buildings} buildings from OpenStreetMap via Overture Maps along 7 km of shore, on Geoscience Australia's 5 m LiDAR terrain. No openly licensed aerial photo covers the bayside, so the ground is drawn from the open map data.","cam":[-3300,-2300,2400],"tgt":[300,500,0],"date":[1,15],"from":"16:00","to":"17:00","hold":8},{"title":"St Kilda Pier and the breakwater","text":"The pier was rebuilt and reopened in December 2024. The breakwater beyond it was built for the sailing at the 1956 Olympics, and about 1,400 little penguins now live in its rocks. They come ashore after sunset.","cam":[-1180,2780,140],"tgt":[-850,3060,0],"date":[10,28],"from":"19:20","to":"20:20","hold":10},{"title":"Luna Park","text":"Opened in December 1912 with the Scenic Railway, the oldest roller coaster in the world that has run without a break: 967 m of track, 16 m high. Mr Moon's face stands at the gate.","cam":[310,2860,95],"tgt":[110,2650,8],"date":[1,15],"from":"17:00","to":"18:00","hold":9},{"title":"Where the heights come from","text":"OpenStreetMap maps the height of only a few buildings in these suburbs, so most here are Microsoft's machine-learning estimates, corrected for their measured bias, or estimates from neighbours. The colours show each building's source.","cam":[1300,1500,900],"tgt":[500,500,0],"date":[1,15],"from":"12:00","to":"13:00","hold":9,"provenance":true},{"title":"The Brighton bathing boxes","text":"96 boxes on Dendy Street Beach, on the Victorian Heritage Register since 2018. Each is built here on its own mapped footprint, facing the bay